# Candidate: project existing detail responses into one selected period

## Problem

Existing `MemberDetails` has member identity, declarations, and a summary whose mandatory `declared_totals` contains all three headlines. `FunderDetails` has identity and headlines at its root. Their remaining fields are historical today. `RankingsRepo::ranking_declarations(start)` rejects historical and full-year requests structurally: SQL only accepts a lower bound. Preserve these established response shapes and exact decimal behavior, but make all fields other than identity and the three mandatory headlines belong to the requested period. Add an explicit `period` discriminator. Do not nest another selected summary or duplicate the selected headline value.

## Usage (caller's view)

```rust
// HTTP boundary: default only when absent; malformed supplied values are 422.
let period = query.period.map(|raw| raw.parse()).transpose()?.unwrap_or_default();
let request = MemberDetailsRequest::new(id.parse()?, period);
let details = state.entity_details_service.member(request).await?;
// details.summary.declared_totals remains complete; all other aggregates and
// details.declarations describe details.period.

// Service: resolve the clock once, obtain complete snapshot, then project.
let context = ReportingContext::at(Utc::now());
let history = repo.member(request.id()).await?.ok_or(NotFound)?;
let details = summarize_member(history, request.period(), context);

// Rankings: no separate date calculation in SQL or aggregation.
let window = context.window(period);
let rows = repo.ranking_declarations(window).await?;
let rankings = summarize_rankings(rows, period, context);
```

```ts
// SvelteKit SSR loader; frontend never interprets period dates.
const period = readPeriod(url.searchParams); // absent -> current-term; invalid -> 400
const result = await loadDetails(route, period, request.signal, fetch, origin);
return { model: { kind: "detail", route, period, result } };
// Rendering uses authoritative headline and backend-filtered data.
const headline = declaredTotal(details.summary.declared_totals, details.period);
// Same helper covers member/funder links, summary/history and ranking categories.
const href = withPeriod(destination, period, { q });
```

## Shape

### Rust domain: one date-policy owner

New `domain/models/reporting_period.rs` owns the vocabulary and period membership. Move existing `CalendarYear` and `CurrentParliamentStart` date semantics here (or remove the latter and retain its one constant inside this module); remove their old alternative membership functions.

```rust
#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, Default, Eq, PartialEq)]
pub enum ReportingPeriod { Historical, #[default] CurrentTerm, CurrentYear }
impl FromStr for ReportingPeriod { type Err = InvalidReportingPeriod; /* exhaustive wire parse */ }
impl Display for ReportingPeriod { /* historical | current-term | current-year */ }

#[derive(Clone, Copy)]
pub struct ReportingContext { calendar_year: CalendarYear }
impl ReportingContext {
    pub fn at(now: DateTime<Utc>) -> Self { todo!() }
    pub fn window(self, period: ReportingPeriod) -> RegistrationWindow { todo!() }
    pub fn calendar_year(self) -> CalendarYear { todo!() }
    pub fn current_term_start(self) -> DateTime<Utc> { todo!() }
}

// Private fields: caller cannot construct a contradictory range or date policy.
#[derive(Clone, Copy)]
pub struct RegistrationWindow {
    start_inclusive: Option<DateTime<Utc>>,
    end_exclusive: Option<DateTime<Utc>>,
}
impl RegistrationWindow {
    pub fn includes(self, registered_at: Option<DateTime<Utc>>) -> bool { todo!() }
    pub fn start(self) -> Option<DateTime<Utc>> { todo!() }
    pub fn end(self) -> Option<DateTime<Utc>> { todo!() }
}
```

Historical resolves to unbounded and includes undated. Term resolves to `2024-07-09T00:00:00Z` onward without upper bound. Current year resolves to UTC January 1 through next January 1, exclusive. `includes` treats missing dates as included only for unbounded windows. Only context can construct these windows. Avoid generic public arbitrary-range builders: history's custom dates remain a frontend refinement.

`MemberDetailsRequest` and `FunderDetailsRequest` gain private `period`, typed constructors, and accessors. Identifier parsing belongs to `MemberId`/`FunderId`; do not keep two request construction pathways with divergent defaults. Service method count stays unchanged. `RankingsService::rankings(&self, period: ReportingPeriod)` receives the same canonical type. `RankingsRepo::ranking_declarations(&self, window: RegistrationWindow)` receives resolved policy rather than reinterpreting the enum or clock.

SQLx query uses optional timestamp bounds with explicitly typed placeholders, preserving current joins and `LEFT JOIN` empty declarations:

```sql
WHERE ($1::timestamptz IS NULL OR d.registration_date >= $1)
  AND ($2::timestamptz IS NULL OR d.registration_date < $2)
```

Currency whitespace becomes the third argument. Both bounds null includes undated; bounded comparisons exclude them. Filtering precedes service grouping and top-ten truncation. Keep existing member all-category aggregation and donation/support restriction for the other boards.

### Existing details as selected projection

Add `period: ReportingPeriod` to `MemberDetails`, `FunderDetails`, and `Rankings`; serialize its slug. Keep all existing fields and their JSON locations. Update old complete-history comments.

```rust
fn summarize_member(history: MemberHistory, period: ReportingPeriod,
                    context: ReportingContext) -> MemberDetails { todo!() }
fn summarize_funder(funding: FunderFunding, period: ReportingPeriod,
                    context: ReportingContext) -> FunderDetails { todo!() }
```

Before retaining records, observe the complete fetched history in `DeclaredTotalsAggregate`, updated to use context's windows for each of the three mandatory totals. Then retain only selected-window declarations. Derive every other summary from that retained list, including known currency/category totals, counts, unknown coverage, first/last dates, undated counts, party groups, recipient rankings, and timelines. For members, use a `PeriodAggregate` over the retained list rather than exposing internals of the three-headline aggregate. For funders, perform the existing recipient/party pass over retained declarations. Profiles and canonical alias/family identity always remain complete. This concentrates completeness in one obvious projection point; future summary fields copied from the nearest loop are scoped automatically. No new repository reads or partial historical inputs are required for details.

### Frontend

`lib/reporting-period.ts` owns frontend's canonical typed vocabulary, labels and selection mapping. Prefer one const object with keys as `ReportingPeriod`; derive options/validation from it. Its exhaustive `declaredTotal(totals, period)` returns an existing mandatory `DeclaredGbpPeriodTotal` field without adding, converting or parsing money. Backend parsing stays in `lib/server`; strict response parsing requires the discriminator and all three headline fields. Confirm returned period equals requested period to reject accidentally mixed fixtures/contracts.

`withPeriod(destination, period, context)` is the one href policy used by existing `memberHref`, `funderHref`, `rankingRows`, summary/history links and category links. It preserves explicit selected period across entities and pages and the existing q context. Period-switch links clone the current URL, set period and clear date refinements and pagination. Do not copy arbitrary query parameters into cross-entity links. Existing local custom date controls filter only history records already selected by backend; reset their state and pagination when the selected period changes, preferably using a keyed history component. The selected headline and summaries remain period totals; custom dates refine the history list only.

Use existing shared `ViewLinks`/`ViewLink` native anchors. If the required compact selector appearance is missing, add a neutral typed variant to those components, document and export the typed capability, and add its gallery/test example. A feature `PeriodSelector.svelte` builds its three link presentation models; shared UI receives labels, hrefs, current state only. Render once after identity/heading and before selected GBP metric on all target views. Change `DeclaredTotals.svelte` to render the selected backend-owned total, not three metrics. All amount strings and coverage semantics remain unchanged.

## Interface depth and red-flag screen

The public growth is one enum, one resolved private-construction window, one parameter per read, and one response discriminator. A caller need only choose a period, not coordinate a headline query and scoped-data query or unpack another response envelope. Date policy is hidden behind one module. Existing repository/service boundaries remain justified by persistence and domain policy, not new pass-through layers. No selected-total duplication, hand-maintained date constants, new cache, or arbitrary bag of optional dates. One wire slug contract necessarily spans Rust and TypeScript; contract tests exercise all three slugs and invalid values.

## Tradeoffs accepted

- We accept changing meaning of existing summary/declaration fields on the default request to current term, in exchange for the requested default and minimal JSON churn. Explicit historical recovers old data semantics.
- We accept reading complete detail history to preserve all mandatory headlines from one snapshot, in exchange for avoiding multiple queries or a nested payload carrying three full detail projections.
- We accept a separate selected member aggregate pass in exchange for clear scoping and no mutable access to headline aggregate internals.

## Alternatives considered

A nested `{ identity, declared_totals, selected: { period, declarations, summary } }` makes structural ownership more explicit but relocates virtually every consumer field, and does not eliminate the need to keep complete input until all headlines are known. Its larger interface and migration surface buy little when the whole response is one period projection. A three-view response would eliminate requests on period changes, but computes and transfers unused detail lists and complicates mandatory vs selected data ownership. Client filtering loses authority for rankings and pre-truncated recipients, so cannot satisfy the requirement.

## Validation

Freeze context at a year boundary. Verify historical undated inclusion; term inclusion at exact midnight; year inclusion through December 31 and exclusion at next January 1, including future-in-current-year dates. Every selected headline must equal its original complete-history headline, independently of response period. Include unknown categories, declaration with no entries, identical funding occurrences, unknown amounts/currencies, known foreign currencies, zero/negative and >JS-safe amounts. Verify funder recipient top-ten changes after filtering and ranking top-ten/category rules. SSR tests assert API period propagation, default and invalid URL behavior, selected metrics, breakdown/history consistency, cross-entity and view navigation, period-change clearing/reset, and native keyboard/mobile selector behavior. Run required Rust checks and frontend check/build/affected Playwright; update SQLx metadata if tracked. No migration.

## Synthesis decision

Candidate offered as the minimal response-projection base; orchestrator decides synthesis.

## Implementation reconciliation

No implementation performed.

## Open questions and risks

Does any existing consumer rely on omitted-period detail calls returning historical data? No compatibility evidence in inspected in-repo callers justifies retaining the contrary default; update all fixtures and default tests together. Are any displayed dates currently intended as stable identity metadata? Treat first/last registration statistics as selected-period data under the user's all-data scope requirement, retaining only actual profile identity unfiltered.

## Next implementation step

Implement the canonical period/context/window domain module and its boundary tests, then thread one typed period through HTTP, services, SQLx ranking input, response parsing and SSR before editing UI.
